# Background Remover is cross-origin isolated so ONNX Runtime can run multi-threaded:
# COOP + COEP are what earn a document SharedArrayBuffer, and without it the WASM
# build stays on one thread. Measured on a 2-core/4-thread laptop, same image:
# inference 13.3s on one thread, 9.2s on two, 8.8s on four (medians of five).
#
# BOTH rules below are required. Isolating the page alone is what broke the tool
# twice in production (2026-09). A COEP: require-corp document may only start a
# dedicated worker whose script response carries COEP itself; otherwise Chromium
# blocks the script (net::ERR_BLOCKED_BY_RESPONSE,
# coep-frame-resource-needs-coep-header). The worker never runs, its error event
# has no message, and the single-threaded retry loads the same script and dies the
# same way. It looked like a hardware-dependent crash only because the local test
# servers used to "verify" it sent COEP on every response, worker script included.
#
# Only the Background Remover worker gets COEP. It fetches nothing cross-origin,
# and the pthread workers ONNX Runtime starts from blob: URLs inherit its policy.
# The other workers under /_astro/ keep their current behaviour.
#
# Exactly ONE rule may match each of these URLs for each header. Cloudflare merges
# every matching rule, and a policy sent twice joins into `same-origin, same-origin`,
# which is invalid, so the browser falls back to unsafe-none and nothing is
# isolated. `/tools/background-remover/*` already covers the trailing-slash URL; do
# not add `/tools/background-remover/` beside it. scripts/validate-site-integrity.mjs
# checks all of this against the build.
/tools/background-remover/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp

/_astro/background-removal.worker-*.js
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp
